From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 14:00:00 +0000
Subject: cpuidle: psci: register no idle states when the firmware has no
 CPU_SUSPEND

PSCI 1.0 and later firmware reports through PSCI_FEATURES whether it
implements CPU_SUSPEND. psci_init_cpu_suspend() already asks, but only
keeps the feature flags of a positive answer; NOT_SUPPORTED is dropped.
The PSCI cpuidle driver then registers every idle state the device tree
describes, although the firmware rejects each CPU_SUSPEND call.

This happens when a device tree with idle states meets a firmware
without CPU_SUSPEND, for example a TF-A platform port that has no
pwr_domain_suspend handler (TF-A then answers PSCI_FEATURES and the call
itself with NOT_SUPPORTED), or a kernel and DTB update on a board that
still runs an older firmware.

A rejected entry returns at once, without WFI. Measured on an Orange Pi
Zero 2 (Allwinner H616) with TF-A lts-v2.14.11 and one idle state in the
DT: about 39000 rejected entries per second on every idle CPU, 92 % of
the idle time spent in no idle state at all. Suspend-to-idle takes the
deepest state without asking the governor, so the CPUs loop through
rejected entries for the whole suspend.

Remember the NOT_SUPPORTED answer and do not register the cpuidle driver
in that case, with one line in the log. The CPUs then idle in WFI through
the default idle routine, as they do without idle states in the DT.
Nothing changes when CPU_SUSPEND is supported, nor for firmware older
than PSCI 1.0, which has no PSCI_FEATURES call.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 drivers/firmware/psci/psci.c   | 13 +++++++++++++
 include/linux/psci.h           |  1 +
 3 files changed, 26 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c
+++ b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c
@@ -400,6 +400,18 @@ static int psci_idle_init_cpu(struct device *dev, int cpu)
 	if (ret <= 0)
 		return ret ? : -ENODEV;
 
+	/*
+	 * The firmware rejects every CPU_SUSPEND call it does not implement.
+	 * An idle state that can never be entered must not be registered: the
+	 * governor would keep selecting it, and suspend-to-idle always takes
+	 * the deepest state, so the CPU would spin through failed entries
+	 * instead of resting in WFI.
+	 */
+	if (!psci_has_cpu_suspend()) {
+		pr_info("CPU_SUSPEND not supported by the firmware, DT idle states ignored\n");
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * Initialize PSCI idle states.
 	 */
diff --git a/drivers/firmware/psci/psci.c b/drivers/firmware/psci/psci.c
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/firmware/psci/psci.c
+++ b/drivers/firmware/psci/psci.c
@@ -77,6 +77,7 @@ struct psci_0_1_function_ids get_psci_0_1_function_ids(void)
 				PSCI_1_0_EXT_POWER_STATE_TYPE_MASK)
 
 static u32 psci_cpu_suspend_feature;
+static bool psci_cpu_suspend_not_supported;
 static bool psci_system_reset2_supported;
 static bool psci_system_off2_hibernate_supported;
 
@@ -91,6 +92,16 @@ bool psci_has_osi_support(void)
 	return psci_cpu_suspend_feature & PSCI_1_0_OS_INITIATED;
 }
 
+/*
+ * False only when the firmware said, through PSCI_FEATURES, that it does not
+ * implement CPU_SUSPEND. Firmware older than PSCI 1.0 has no PSCI_FEATURES
+ * call and cannot say so, hence CPU_SUSPEND is assumed to work there.
+ */
+bool psci_has_cpu_suspend(void)
+{
+	return !psci_cpu_suspend_not_supported;
+}
+
 static inline bool psci_power_state_loses_context(u32 state)
 {
 	const u32 mask = psci_has_ext_power_state() ?
@@ -598,6 +609,8 @@ static void __init psci_init_cpu_suspend(void)
 
 	if (feature != PSCI_RET_NOT_SUPPORTED)
 		psci_cpu_suspend_feature = feature;
+	else
+		psci_cpu_suspend_not_supported = true;
 }
 
 /*
diff --git a/include/linux/psci.h b/include/linux/psci.h
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/include/linux/psci.h
+++ b/include/linux/psci.h
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@ int psci_cpu_suspend_enter(u32 state);
 bool psci_power_state_is_valid(u32 state);
 int psci_set_osi_mode(bool enable);
 bool psci_has_osi_support(void);
+bool psci_has_cpu_suspend(void);
 
 struct psci_operations {
 	u32 (*get_version)(void);
-- 
Armbian

